## Sync: the same work on your other machines

Sync is optional and sits on top of everything above: it carries the op log
between your instances through a relay. Nothing else here needs it.

  connect <url>       remember a relay and push (do this once)
  sync                push then pull -- what you run day to day
  apps enable sync    set and forget: an auto-starting daemon syncs every 30s
  config set sync.branches all
                      sync moves every branch too, not just main
  push / pull         one direction
  sync status         are we in sync? (cheap, no transfer)
  whoami              this instance's id
  identity <name>     name it something readable

  branch push <name>  put a branch on the relay
  branch pull         bring your branches down on another machine

  review pull [url] [queue]     stage someone's ops instead of applying them
  review import <file> [queue]  same, from a bundle on disk
  review <queue>                what's waiting in it
  review approve <queue>        fold the queue into main
  review reject <queue>         discard it

A queue IS a branch, named by you (default "review"), so every branch verb
works on it: `diff <queue>`, `log <queue>`, `conflicts branch <queue>`. The
staged count is what's actually pending, not how much of the bundle was new to
you -- a whole-log bundle is mostly ops you already have.

DECISIONS TRAVEL, not just code. Version restores and constraint acks are
ops, so they travel with their reasons.

Branches are keyed by owner, so a branch follows YOU across machines: give
both instances the same `identity`.

